You're on the right track with your approach, but there's a small issue with how the **nightmode layer** is being applied. The current setup uses a `label` with a `:before` pseudo-element that becomes visible when the checkbox is checked. However, this `label` is **not a full overlay** — it's just a semi-transparent background that sits on top of the content, but it **does not cover the entire screen** or **prevent interaction with the content behind it**.

To make the **nightmode layer** cover the entire screen and **prevent interaction with the content behind it**, you need to use a **full-screen overlay** (like a `div` with `position: fixed`, `top: 0`, `left: 0`, `width: 100%`, `height: 100%`) and **make it non-interactive** (i.e., `pointer-events: none` when not active, and `pointer-events: auto` when active).

Here's how you can **modify your code** to achieve this:

---

### ✅ **Solution: Add a Full-Screen Overlay for Nightmode**

Add a new `div` inside your body that will act as the **nightmode overlay**. This overlay will be hidden by default and shown when the checkbox is checked.

---

### ✅ **Updated HTML (Add this inside the body)**

```html
<div id="nightmode-overlay" class="nightmode-overlay">
  <p>Energy saving nightmode layer</p>
</div>
```

---

### ✅ **Updated CSS (Add this to your style section)**

```css
.nightmode-overlay {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
  color: white;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  z-index: 1000;
  pointer-events: none; /* Prevents interaction with content behind */
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.3s ease-out;
}

.nightmode-overlay.active {
  opacity: 1;
  pointer-events: auto; /* Allows interaction with overlay content */
}
```

